WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.090
Film Courage: Why are limitations on films actually a good thing in your mind?

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:10.170
Adam Davis, Writer/Director: Because that is what brings up
creativity having limitations on a film

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:14.460
set
brings out creativity because when

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:21.090
you're sort of stuck by these boxes
whether they're financial or you know

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:27.359
location based or time based the best
stuff really really comes out of you

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:32.820
like you're able to think of things that
maybe you wouldn't have you know if you

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:37.230
had all the resources in the world it
can be over indulgent but when you don't

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:42.510
have any resources then you really have
to get crafty and that's when the most

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:48.570
and the purest creativity comes out and
what I've found is that things are much

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:53.489
better when there are rules and sort of
borders that you have to sort of work

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:59.160
your way around versus you can do
anything so you know you end up you know

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:05.430
potentially with nothing that's true
like maybe a cool backdrop but then the

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:11.939
story suffers with broken ceiling
you had one location you had it for four

00:01:11.939 --> 00:01:16.320
days you had all your actors there you
knew like you didn't have to really

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.200
change locations were there other
limitations within that within that

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:26.420
office building where you were shooting
that really lends itself to the story I

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:33.720
think you know we had a little bit of
free rein of that office which was great

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:42.270
it was just the limitation of time was
something that everybody runs into but

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:48.030
for us you know we had like a monitor
sitting there going like like you have

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:53.340
to be out by a certain time you know you
can't go over this so that was something

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.270
that you know kept us going because it's
like we only have this much time in the

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:02.340
day you know we're gonna shoot for maybe
10 12 hours but like that's it we can't

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.140
keep shooting we can't go over like we
don't have that luxury so with the time

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:11.730
constraint you know that was very
motivating and and helped everybody in

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:15.990
plus you know
the story itself had just lended itself

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:20.070
to that because I wanted them to feel
like they were in a high-pressure

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:24.950
situation so the fact that we were in
reality in a high-pressure situation

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:30.960
really really helped you know get that
feeling on the actors because they're on

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.620
a conference call on a Sunday mm-hmm and
they all have to kind of go and face

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:41.940
each other yeah interesting and how many
pages did you do a day I think the most

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:45.810
we ever did in one day was about 60
pages oh wow

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:54.800
which is insane and unheard of if we did
like you know between 80 and 90 setups

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.490
which is crazy but again totally doable
because we didn't have to move lights

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:04.290
you know using the all natural lighting
but yeah like we we were definitely

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:09.090
cranking through and getting it and you
know it's it's a tough thing to do when

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:13.290
you're in a conference room for the
majority of your running time you just I

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:17.640
remember thinking how do I make sure
that people aren't bored because that's

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:22.290
the most important thing because it can
be so easy so like visually I have these

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:27.390
four walls right those are the
parameters like what can I do to really

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:32.459
make this one looking just look
interesting but make sure that it really

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:37.350
gets across what the characters were
going through visually which was a hard

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:41.580
thing to do but but something I think we
were able to accomplish did you do a lot

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:47.040
of close-ups then of just like eye
movement or like how did you yeah it was

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.910
a variation of not using too much you
know close-ups but when it was necessary

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:59.790
using that using movement when the
moment or the scene called for it you

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:05.430
know there's a specific moment that I
can recall where you've got Ken you know

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:08.880
the boss sitting in the center of the
conference room and then you've got

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:14.160
Tyler and and Garrett on the sides and
they're sort of going back and forth you

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:17.400
know like sniping at each other so it's
like if you have three of them right

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:22.140
here how do I make this dynamic and
interesting and show the interplay

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:27.470
between the characters so what I came up
with is having the camera throw

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:31.730
from character to character and sort of
a one-take so you know we would have

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:35.150
Tyler say something and then we would
wit pin it over to Garrett who would say

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:37.790
something we would go back and then we
would go to Ken and then back and forth

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:42.650
so you go oh they are you know volleying
off of each other almost plain of verbal

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:47.210
tennis you know so you see that and that
would play so much more different had I

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:51.380
decided to do just the standard you know
like single shot here single shot

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:55.790
they're cutting back and forth to really
like grounded in one shot and move it

00:04:55.790 --> 00:05:00.710
back and forth gave it much more of a
sense of energy and a connectedness that

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:04.220
I don't think you know just standard
sort of coming back and forth would have

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:08.930
done did the actors know that at one
point you would do sixty pages in one

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:16.220
day of your four days shoes oh no of
course but it's just what we were able

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:20.450
to do which was great I let him know
that we were gonna be shooting a lot and

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:24.260
we had to get a lot done but I even I
didn't think that we were gonna shoot

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:28.970
that much like I didn't plan for like
let's do sixty pages like that's insane

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:33.680
I was like okay let's we got to get at
least twenty maybe thirty you know four

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:39.290
days we'll stitch together ninety
minutes but but we were just in it you

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:44.660
know and and they were down for it and
they wanted to keep going and you know

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:50.210
it was exciting because everybody was
sort of pushing towards that same goal

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:55.280
of like let's get this done and because
you know the the fact that they're all

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:58.910
stuck in this room together
and sort of like at each other's throats

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:02.840
it's like it absolutely helped that we
were just barreling it through it

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:09.320
because it didn't give them time to
think or reflect back on online readings

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:15.500
or performances like they were really in
the moment which is what was necessary

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:19.790
for it and I think we got the best
performances out of it because of that

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:24.919
constant sort of like striving and
pushing when day four came toward the

00:06:24.919 --> 00:06:28.490
end of the day how are those last few
hours and then when when they finally

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:32.840
said okay it's time like how are you
loading the truck well what's that like

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:39.650
sure there were no trucks didn't have
money for trucks okay it was a Camry

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:43.660
what
yeah floated up into the car and do that

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:49.210
and like find some place to throw out
trash that was interesting the fact that

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:53.919
you know at this location like we had to
bring trash bags and we had to take the

00:06:53.919 --> 00:06:58.270
trash with us so trying to figure out in
Los Angeles where what where can I

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:03.250
legally throw away trash was something
that we had to do that I had to do you

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:07.210
know after each shooting day so just
another thing that a director has to do

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:12.600
is drive around with trash in their back
seat and throw it out at some place but

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:23.490
could you take it to the dump no no we
felt like some open you know trash bins

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:29.500
which other the four days on broken
ceiling was the 60 page 60 pages a with

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:33.520
that that was the second day the second
day was the one that we did the 60 pages

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:38.860
I think the first day we did around 30
and then the second day we did around

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:45.820
around 60 or so and yeah it's funny
because we did so much stuff like that

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:50.910
first weekend and then I was able to go
back and just you know I had a full week

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:55.780
to sort of look at the footage and I
started editing right away and I found

00:07:55.780 --> 00:07:59.830
certain things that we had missed or
that we didn't get or that you know

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:03.970
there was like a boom shadow in some of
the shots and like stuff that on the day

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:08.919
like we hadn't caught so it was it was
super invaluable and I'm so glad that we

00:08:08.919 --> 00:08:12.729
split it up into two chunks because I
was able to look at everything and go

00:08:12.729 --> 00:08:17.110
okay like we actually need to reshoot
this and we need you know pick up with

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:21.610
that and we got to do this so when the
next weekend came around I had built in

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:25.240
you know into the shooting schedule all
of these things that we had to to sort

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:29.950
of reshoot which was an interesting
challenge but everybody you know sort of

00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:36.039
rose up to that but then on the the sort
of final days and the final day

00:08:36.039 --> 00:08:42.279
specifically it was down to all of the
Karen Kendrick stuff you know for her

00:08:42.279 --> 00:08:46.120
character Angela because she there's a
thread through the movie where she's

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:51.070
sort of you know we see her on her own
which is very interesting and very fun

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:53.440
in cinematic because we got to venture
outside of the car

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:58.829
room and sort of travel you know
throughout other parts of the office and

00:08:58.829 --> 00:09:03.850
it was cool because everybody else was
sort of gone that day it was actually I

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:08.860
think it was Father's Day or it was one
of the holidays where a couple of crew

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:14.290
members were like I couldn't can't make
it you know I think we we lost our

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:18.220
script supervisor or whatever on that
Dame one of her producers but it was

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:22.629
alright because it was mostly like non
dialogue based he was more visual so it

00:09:22.629 --> 00:09:29.139
was cool and because we got to shoot the
final shot of the movie as sort of the

00:09:29.139 --> 00:09:34.540
last thing that we actually shot so it
was nice you know anyway it was calming

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:40.029
after all of that you know long dialogue
and work to sort of spend a day shooting

00:09:40.029 --> 00:09:47.110
non dialogue based visual stuff it was
cool at the end of the day the for four

00:09:47.110 --> 00:09:50.949
days did a representative from the
building come up and be like okay guys

00:09:50.949 --> 00:09:56.560
this is it I mean we gotta wrap this up
or how does that work for that we had a

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:01.959
monitor that was kind of there the whole
time so yeah there wasn't no like you

00:10:01.959 --> 00:10:06.430
know you guys got to wrap it up by that
time we knew that like we have to go but

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:12.100
it was cool in that like the last day we
didn't shoot the full amount it was a

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:16.209
little bit of a shorter day so we left a
little bit early and it was still you

00:10:16.209 --> 00:10:20.199
know he was still sunny out the Sun was
actually coming down you know Magic Hour

00:10:20.199 --> 00:10:26.649
was it was pretty good and it was very
emotional I remember it and this is

00:10:26.649 --> 00:10:31.209
something that for some reason and maybe
it's just the way that I handle you know

00:10:31.209 --> 00:10:37.750
being done with a shoot but I remember
you know going to my car you know the

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:43.540
car with a bunch of trash bags and the
trunk and and and I drove home and I sat

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:48.459
there in the garage and I took about I
don't know maybe five ten minutes and I

00:10:48.459 --> 00:10:54.970
just sort of like started crying like
happy tears happy that we had done it

00:10:54.970 --> 00:11:03.189
sort of relieved that we had done it but
just so grateful that everybody had

00:11:03.189 --> 00:11:07.420
jumped in to do this crazy thing she did
in a feature film in four days

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:10.420
shooting 60 pages in one day like that's
insane

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:14.800
but everybody hopped on board and they
did it and just like it was all the

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:19.420
emotions of like gratitude and
thankfulness that came out and yeah it

00:11:19.420 --> 00:11:24.160
was it was a great feeling and again one
of those feelings that that I love to

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:28.590
have and that's why I'm still you know
making films

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:36.370
you

